Example:
=========
How I would upgrade RHEL version from one to another
-----------------------------------------------------
I would use the Leapp utility, which is Red Hat’s supported in-place upgrade framework for major version upgrades
from RHEL 9 to RHEL 10

1. First, I verify prerequisites
=================================
Before starting the upgrade, I check:
                                /var must have 10GB free(leap stores the data in /var/lib/leapp)
                                / must have 4 - 5 GB free
                                /tmp must be mount with exec
                                Backups are mandatory(Recommended RedHat)
                                Current OS version
                                Active Red Hat subscription
                                Repository availability
                                Hardware and application compatibility
                                Third-party packages and kernel modules
                      Example commands:
                                        cat /etc/redhat-release
                                        subscription-manager status
                                        dnf repolist
                                        uname -r
I also verify whether applications support RHEL 10 because some drivers or packages may not be compatible after upgrade.

2. Take backup and rollback plan
=================================
Before any major upgrade, I ensure rollback capability.
        For VMs:  Take VMware/Hyper-V snapshot
        For physical servers: Take filesystem backup using rsync/tar or enterprise backup tools
Example:    tar -cvpzf backup.tar.gz /etc /var/www /home
I also validate that recovery actually works.

3. Update existing RHEL 9 system
=================================
I first patch the current OS fully because Red Hat recommends upgrading from the latest supported minor release.

sudo dnf update -y
sudo reboot

Then cleanup unused packages and old kernels:

sudo dnf clean all
sudo dnf autoremove

4. Install Leapp utility
==========================
Then I install the Leapp upgrade tool:  
                                    sudo dnf install -y leapp-upgrade

5. Run pre-upgrade assessment
==============================
This is the most important phase.
                                sudo leapp preupgrade --target 10.0
Leapp generates reports under:
                                /var/log/leapp/leapp-report.txt

I analyze:
        Unsupported packages
        Deprecated drivers
        Removed kernel modules
        SELinux issues
        Repository conflicts

Then I remediate all inhibitors before proceeding.

6. Perform the upgrade
=======================
Once the precheck passes:       sudo leapp upgrade --target 10.0

After packages are downloaded, I reboot the server:   sudo reboot

The system boots into a special upgrade initramfs environment and completes the migration automatically.


7. Post-upgrade validation
===========================
After reboot, I validate:   
                        cat /etc/redhat-release
                        uname -r
                        systemctl --failed

I also verify:
          Application services
          Network connectivity
          SELinux status
          Mount points
          Subscription status

Example:
      sestatus
      df -h
      ip a


